Z||000000||GTDude||12-16-2001||11:20 AM||||Hey guyz.  I've always been skeptical of synthetic oils.....but im starting to come around.  I'd like to know what you guys use and why.  I've got a 1986 GT 2.8 with no internal engine mods.  Thanks in advance.<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT<p>[This message has been edited by GTDude (edited 12-16-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||REDHOTT88||12-16-2001||11:32 AM||||This issue was addressed a lot in previous posts...<P>And the winner is.. Mobil 1 try-synthetic!<P>Check out previous posts in the "search"<P>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">       <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||12
Z||000002||GMGW3||12-16-2001||12:57 PM||||Well  Ive been using Mobil-1 synthetic for years now, and wont go back to anything else.<BR>the mains reasons why are as follows.<P>- Handles heat much better, wont sludge up.<BR>- flows much better at sub zero.<BR>- superior friction properteys<BR>- over all just a clean running oil.<P>I had a nieghbor that pulled a part a engine for a upgrade rebuild, and had been using mobil-1 for 110k miles in it. There was no sludge or varnish build up anywhere in it, the cylinder walls had no ridge, and cam lobes looked almost like new.  That pretty much sold me on it.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTDude||12-16-2001||01:26 PM||||Thanks guys......that's the oil I was considering believe it or not.  This kinda clenches it for me!  Merry Xmas!<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||10
Z||000004||artherd||12-16-2001||09:43 PM||||Mobil 1 all the way!<P>I just pulled apart a Northstar with 20k on it... it looked NEW... I mean, no heat marks anywhere, the inside of the thing was "clean!" shiney and you could barely tell where the cam maching marks had worn smooth during break-in.  barely.  Then nothing else happened.<P>Great stuff.  not good, GREAT!<P>It's got a MIL part number even. Supposedly it is used to lubricate some components of F16 TurboJet engines.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||ka4nkf||12-17-2001||12:31 AM||||Synthetic is great if you live in a place where temperatures are hot, or you are racing and your engine gets hot. otherwise regular oil is ok. we bought a vacuum pump at my work place when I was working and this pump turned very high RPM and got very hot. the manufacturer guaranteed this pump for one full year if we put Mobil-1 in it. otherwise it was only guaranteed 30 day's with reg oil.<BR>it is good stuff. just don't put it in a car with high mileage because you will have lot's of leaks||||reg||1
Z||000006||L67Formula||12-17-2001||01:07 AM||||[QUOTE]I'd like to know what you guys use and why.<P>Mobil, Havoline, Valvoline, Castrol. Whichever is on sale at Walmart in the 5qt jug. I buy lots of it when I find good deals and I can't tell that any one is better than any other. Any synthetic is better than any nonsynthetic oil in my opinion. Even Walmart's house brand(made by Quaker State) is good.<BR>jp<P>||||reg||1
